#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152970} :activity/id #uuid "50b01990-2284-52a1-b046-89a46d877bf3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048864 :topic/id #uuid "c788c893-5c00-5b28-be4c-bd65dc3ed101" :topic/math-academy-id 2789} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153433 :tutorial/content "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are **disjoint** if they have no element in common. In other words, their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 5,10 \\}\n$$\n\n \n\nThese sets are disjoint since their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\} \\cap \\{ 5,10 \\} = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $C=(-\\infty,5]$ and $D=(-2,\\infty)$ are *not* disjoint since the intersection of these sets is *not* empty:\n\n$$\nC\\cap D = (-\\infty,5] \\cap (-2,\\infty) = (-2,5] \\neq \\emptyset\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "92860310-1be6-50cf-8447-2b24a95db5e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4c6c5e7f-d5d8-5038-8ce3-1957ac96ecd0" :step/math-academy-id 157019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151817 :question/id #uuid "841e5dcb-c092-5159-bfc4-d6580999c742" :question/math-academy-id "e-12760"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534020}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets"} :step/id #uuid "0ba98889-a41c-532a-bc68-6e4b156abe4a" :step/math-academy-id 157020 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153434 :tutorial/content "The **disjoint union** of two sets is defined differently depending on whether the sets are non-overlapping (disjoint) or overlapping (have a non-empty intersection). We'll use the special symbol \"$\\sqcup$\" (square cup) to denote the disjoint union. \n\nThe disjoint union of two non-overlapping (disjoint) sets $A$ and $B$ is simply the union of the sets.\n\nFor example, since \n\n$$\n\\{ 0,2,4 \\} \\cap \\{ 1, 3 \\} = \\emptyset,\n$$\n\nwe find the disjoint union of these sets as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{0,2,4\\}⊔\\{1,3\\} & =\\{0,2,4\\}\\cup \\{1,3\\} \\\\  & =\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d264ae6-080d-5ca0-be06-b6d41a5f4aa7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8005 :tutorial/title "The Disjoint Union of Non-Overlapping Sets"} :step/id #uuid "a0fe9f92-abdf-5afd-94bd-7e0416495b3f" :step/math-academy-id 157023 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152973}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152102 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151818 :question/id #uuid "ea87c6f2-d0ef-5f8d-8f87-b9219272cedd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12761"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7079378e-2cec-550d-a647-405a0acb9b61" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425787}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Disjoint Union of Non-Overlapping Sets"} :step/id #uuid "7342dbf9-8375-507b-9fcc-03ddc4dd27a7" :step/math-academy-id 157024 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153436 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the following overlapping sets: \n\n$$\nA_1 = \\{ \\square, \\triangle \\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{ \\heartsuit, \\square\\}.\n$$\n\nThese sets overlap because their intersection is non-empty. \n\n$$\nA_1 \\cap A_2 = \\{ \\square \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTo construct the disjoint union of these overlapping sets, we proceed as follows:\n\n- For each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is \"tagged\" with the corresponding index:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}=\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}=\\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- As a result, the new sets $A_1^\\ast$ and $A_2^\\ast$ are non-overlapping. So, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & =\\{◻,△\\}⊔\\{♡,◻\\} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\}\\cup \\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1),(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e3b30b0-95ac-5b38-a7e6-169cc3409776" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8137 :tutorial/title "The Disjoint Union of Overlapping Sets"} :step/id #uuid "233824ca-a0f9-5e23-af03-ba0c108fc90c" :step/math-academy-id 157027 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152103 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151819 :question/id #uuid "65852684-d9ce-5830-b95f-9abae3dadd4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12762"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5188a6-60a0-5e38-9164-201c3eb60cd5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425788}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Disjoint Union of Overlapping Sets"} :step/id #uuid "d572a9fc-16a3-5783-b7e1-a3d5a6eab501" :step/math-academy-id 157028}] :activity/title "Disjoint Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152373}]}
